.TH CLOG_ERROR 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
clog_error \- emit a log line at ERROR level with a backtrace
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/clogger.h>

clog_error(clog lg, const char *fmt, ...);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Writes a log line at CLOG_ERROR level and picks up __FILE__, __LINE__
and __func__ for you.  After the structured log line it appends a
stack backtrace as continuation lines indented by a tab:

  ts=... level=ERROR src=file.c:42 func=handle msg="db timeout"
  \et#0 ./myapp(handle+0x1a) [0x7f...]
  \et#1 ./myapp(main+0x42) [0x7f...]

A continuation line does not start with "ts=", which lets a log
aggregator group it with the ERROR record above it.

Backtrace frames 0 and 1 belong to the logger itself and are skipped, so
frame #0 is the caller of clog_error.  A backtrace has at most 64
frames.

The binary must be linked with -rdynamic for the symbols to resolve.
The capture itself can fail, on a platform with no execinfo.h or when an
allocation fails for a moment while symbol names are resolved.  The
logger then writes the log line as usual and appends one
"\et#error backtrace unavailable" continuation line in place of the
frames, so you can see that the backtrace is missing.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
lg     Logger handle (clog).
fmt    printf-style format string.
\&...    Format arguments (optional).
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
clog_error does NOT stop the process; use clog_fatal when you want the
process to stop.
The shared write lock covers the backtrace write as well, so the
backtrace and its log line always appear together, even when several
threads log at the same time.
When memory stays short for a while, or the content of a record leaves
little room before the internal buffer cap, a backtrace frame that
cannot be formatted is left out, while a shorter frame after it can
still go out.  An incomplete frame is never written, so every line that
goes out is a complete, well-formed record.  When not a single frame
fits, one "\et#error backtrace unavailable" continuation line takes
their place, the same marker that a failed capture gives.  Under the
same pressure in CLOG_FMT_JSON mode the backtrace can fail to fit at
all, and a "bt_error":"unavailable" key then takes the place of "bt",
so the omission is visible there too.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
clog_error(lg, "failed to open %s: %s", path, strerror(errno));
clog_error(lg, "unexpected state %d in FSM", state);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR clog_alert (3),
.BR clog_fatal (3),
.BR clog_warn (3),
.BR clog_create_from_fd_mp (3)
